#
#  Test: nested '@reference' sections.
#
#  This file is $INCLUDEd inside "modules { }", so '@..' names the root
#  section of the configuration.  Each '@..' below sits at the top level of
#  its own section, which is where an '@reference' is allowed.
#
#  cf_file.c keeps one frame->at_reference slot per stack frame.  An inner
#  reference overwrites the outer one, so only the innermost is ever
#  restored.  The closing braces after that walk up the reference target's
#  ancestry instead of the file's own nesting.  Here that walk reaches the
#  root section and reads its NULL parent, which used to trip the assertion
#  at the top of parse_input().
#
#  This file must parse, and must leave the parser back inside
#  "modules { }" where it started.
#
@.. cf_ref_outer {
	d {
	}

	@.. cf_ref_middle {
		e {
		}

		@.. cf_ref_inner {
		}
	}
}

#
#  Sentinel for where the closing braces above left us.
#
#  "reverse_lookups" is a boolean in the root section, and "xyzzy" is not a
#  boolean.  "modules { }" ignores pairs it does not recognise, so this line
#  is harmless where it belongs, and is a parse error if the braces above
#  moved us somewhere else.  Without it, a fix which stops the crash but
#  still loses track of the current section would pass this test.
#
reverse_lookups = xyzzy
